    "id": "news-1788098780142-0",
    "title": "Dutch Court Sentences Rwandan Man to Life for 1994 Genocide Role",
    "original_title": "Xasuuqii Rwanda: Nin Xabsi Daa’in Lagu Xukumay",
    "slug": "dutch-court-sentences-rwandan-man-to-life-for-1994-genocide-role",
    "summary": "A 66-year-old man, Eugène N., was sentenced to life imprisonment by a court in The Hague on August 28, 2026, for his involvement in the 1994 Rwandan genocide, including attacks against Tutsi civilians.",
    "content": "A Dutch court has handed a life sentence to a 66-year-old man for his role in the 1994 Rwandan genocide, marking the latest effort to bring perpetrators to justice decades after the atrocities. The district court in The Hague convicted Eugène N., a former local official in the Mbazi area of southern Rwanda, on charges of war crimes and genocide. The verdict was delivered on August 28, 2026.\n\nThe court found that Eugène N. actively participated in attacks against two communities that were predominantly Tutsi. During these assaults, homes were looted and some were set ablaze. The most severe incidents occurred in and around the Byi region, where hundreds of civilians were killed. The judges concluded that his actions were deliberate and contributed to the systematic campaign of violence that claimed the lives of an estimated 800,000 Tutsis and moderate Hutus over a period of about 100 days.\n\nEugène N. had been living in the Netherlands for many years before his arrest, thanks to international efforts to track down suspected genocide perpetrators who fled to Europe. His trial is part of a broader pattern of prosecutions in Dutch courts, which have taken up cases under universal jurisdiction. This case also highlights the ongoing work of organizations that document and seek justice for the survivors of the genocide, many of whom have waited over three decades for accountability.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this news carries particular resonance. Many Somali refugees and their descendants have experienced or witnessed similar mass atrocities, and the pursuit of justice for the Rwandan genocide serves as a reminder of the importance of accountability. It also underscores the role that courts in Western countries can play in addressing crimes committed elsewhere, offering hope that those who commit such acts will not escape scrutiny, regardless of where they settle. The verdict reaffirms that the long arm of justice can reach across borders, a principle that holds significance for many displaced communities worldwide.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/30/xasuuqii-rwanda-nin-xabsi-daain-lagu-xukumay/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=xasuuqii-rwanda-nin-xabsi-daain-lagu-xukumay",
